>> title: Saba no Misoni
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: japanese, mackerel, miso, ginger, simmered, fish, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: さばの味噌煮, saba no misoni, saba no misoni, 鯖の味噌煮, サバの味噌煮, mackerel simmered in miso, miso-simmered mackerel
>> pairs-with: gohan, sumashi-jiru
>> servings: 4
>> time: 25 min
>> slack: narrow — mackerel is done at ten minutes and dry at twenty, and the miso goes flat and slightly bitter the moment it is allowed a real boil; the two windows close within five minutes of each other and neither reopens
>> step.1: scald and rinse
>> step.2: simmer 10 min under a drop lid
>> step.3: whisk the miso in, spoon over 5 min

Pour @boiling water{4%cups}(950 mL; straight off the kettle) over @mackerel fillets{1%lb}(450 g; 4 pieces, skin scored once across) sitting in a #colander{}, until the skin goes from blue-black to pale, then rinse them under cold water. This is 霜降り and it is not a wash: it sets the surface so the pieces hold together, and it takes off the smell people think is mackerel and is really just blood.

Simmer @&(~1)scalded mackerel{} in @water{3/4%cup}(180 mL) with @sake{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @mirin{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @sugar{1 1/2%Tbs} and @ginger{1%piece}(1 1/2 in, sliced thin) in a #wide pan{} under a drop lid ~simmer{10%min}, skin-side up and in one layer. The ginger is here to do a job, not for decoration — it is what stops the miso reading as sweet.

Whisk @red miso{3%Tbs}(55 g) into a ladleful of @&(~1)cooking liquid{} until it is smooth, stir it back into the pan, and ~simmer{5%min} at the barest tremble, spooning the liquid over the fish until it coats the back of the spoon.
